module bindings.swift.redirect_cookie_scheme_unchecked;

// URLSession follows HTTP redirects itself, and the one documented point at
// which an app sees the hop is the task delegate
// `urlSession(_:task:willPerformHTTPRedirection:newRequest:completionHandler:)`:
// the new request is handed to the delegate already built for the target the
// 3xx named, and whatever the delegate returns through the completion handler
// is what gets sent. A delegate that reattaches a credential header there --
// `setValue(_:forHTTPHeaderField:)` with `Cookie`, `Authorization` or another
// of the names HTTP authenticates by -- attaches that credential to a URL the
// app did not choose, and the scheme is the part that decides whether the
// attach is safe: the redirect target can be `http://`, and a session cookie
// forwarded over it is transmitted in cleartext, which is what the cookie's
// own Secure attribute forbids. The safe form of this code names the scheme
// before attaching -- `url?.scheme?.lowercased() == "https"` is the idiom,
// URL's documented accessor for exactly this question.
//
// The fact recorded here is read through the tokens the Swift frontend emits
// for a file: one context node per file, so every clause is file-wide. The
// redirect delegate is anchored by its parameter label, the one spelling
// Apple's documentation gives the hook. The credential attach is anchored by
// the header-set call's argument label -- Swift appends a call's argument
// labels to the call node's path as dotted segments, so `forHTTPHeaderField`
// in a call path holds exactly when a header is being set on a request --
// together with a credential header name, the names the HTTP specifications
// fix. The control is the absence of a scheme read anywhere in the file, the
// accessor any independent implementation of the check has to spell.
// Comments produce no tokens, so prose naming the scheme does not silence the
// finding.
//
// Residual, false-negative direction: a scheme read anywhere in the file
// suppresses -- including one made for an unrelated reason, such as a custom
// URL scheme probe in a sibling method -- so a file that handles redirects and
// credentials but reads `scheme` nowhere near them goes unreported. A redirect
// follower built without the delegate (reading a `Location` header by hand)
// does not anchor either. False-positive direction: the credential name is
// matched as a substring of a literal, so a literal naming a
// credential-adjacent string in a file that also sets request headers,
// handles redirects and never reads the scheme reports once for the file.
binding swRedirectReattachCredentialSchemeUnchecked {
  query pattern presenceNode where node.scope == "module"
    and node.context.language == "swift"
    and containsAny(node.context.paramLabel, ["willPerformHTTPRedirection"])
    and containsAny(node.context.callPath, ["forHTTPHeaderField"])
    and containsAny(node.context.literal, ["Cookie", "Cookie2", "Authorization", "Proxy-Authorization", "WWW-Authenticate"])
    and not containsAny(node.context.identifier, ["scheme"])
  emit issue code.CleartextChannel at node
}
